TEST(1)                        P32W Unix                         TEST(1)

NAME
       test, [ - evaluate a condition

SYNOPSIS
       test expr
       [ expr ]

DESCRIPTION
       test evaluates the expression expr and exits with status 0 if
       it is true, 1 if it is false, and 2 (with a message) if expr
       is malformed. It is meant for the if and while of sh(1).
       Called as [, it requires a last argument of ], which it
       removes.

       Each operator and operand must be a separate argument. The
       primaries are:
       -e file
              file exists
       -f file
              file exists and is a regular file
       -d file
              file exists and is a directory
       -c file, -b file, -p file
              file exists and is a character device, a block
              device, a pipe (FIFO)
       -s file
              file exists and is not empty
       -r file
              file is readable by the real user
       -w file
              file is writable by the real user
       -x file
              file is executable (searchable, for a directory)
       -u file, -g file, -k file
              file exists and has its set-user-id, set-group-id,
              sticky bit set
       -h file, -L file
              file is a symbolic link: always false (P32W Unix has
              none)
       -t fd  the file descriptor fd is a terminal
       -z s   the string s is empty
       -n s   the string s is not empty
       s      the string s is not empty
       s1 = s2
              the strings are equal
       s1 != s2
              the strings differ
       s1 < s2, s1 > s2
              s1 sorts before (after) s2, by character codes; the
              < and > must be quoted for the shell
       n1 -eq n2
              the integers are equal; likewise -ne, -lt, -le, -gt
              and -ge for not equal, less than, less or equal,
              greater than, greater or equal. An integer is an
              optional sign and digits (blanks around are allowed),
              from -2147483648 to 2147483647; anything else is an
              error
       f1 -nt f2, f1 -ot f2
              f1 was modified later (earlier) than f2; a file that
              does not exist is older than one that does
       f1 -ef f2
              f1 and f2 are the same file (the same i-node)

       These combine, from highest precedence to lowest:
       ( expr )      grouping; the parentheses must be quoted for
                     the shell, as in \( and \)
       ! expr        true if expr is false
       expr -a expr  true if both are true
       expr -o expr  true if either is true

       As POSIX requires, the number of arguments decides how they
       are read before any operator is looked at, so a string that
       happens to look like an operator is still taken as a string:
       0      false
       1      true if the argument is not empty (so test -f is true)
       2      ! s (s empty), or a unary primary such as -f file
       3      a binary primary (s1 = s2, n1 -lt n2, s1 -a s2 ...),
              else ! with two arguments, else ( s )
       4      ! with three arguments, or ( with two arguments )
       5 or more
              parsed by the precedence above
       So test "$x" = y compares strings even when $x is -f or !.

EXAMPLES
       test -f /etc/passwd && echo yes
       if [ "$USER" = root ]; then echo '#'; fi
       while [ $i -lt 10 ]; do i=`expr $i + 1`; done
       [ -d /mnt/usr -a -w /mnt ]
       [ \( "$a" = x -o "$a" = y \) -a ! -f lock ]

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the expression is true; 1 if it is false or missing; 2
       for an error, with a message on the standard error:
       "missing ]" (for [), "missing )", "x: unary operator
       expected", "x: binary operator expected", "x: unexpected
       argument", "argument expected", "x: integer expression
       expected" or "x: integer out of range".

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), expr(1), stat(2)

BUGS
       With five or more arguments the reading can still be
       ambiguous: an operand that looks like an operator (!, (, -a,
       -o, -f ...) is taken as one where that makes sense. Quote or
       prefix such strings (test "x$a" = xy) when it matters.

       -r, -w and -x ask access(2), so they use the real user id.
       Missing: -O, -G, -S, and the string comparisons are by
       character codes only.
